{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgubvJf7PovUKKiY3ySSf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the route to `route.camel.yaml`. I haven't run it or tested it against a Camel 4.22 runtime.\n\n**What it does:**\n- **Input:** it receives messages on `direct:rejection-mail`.\n- **Recipient:** it copies the `partnerEmail` header into the `To` header. The mail component sends to the address in `To` when that header is set, so each partner gets their own email.\n- **Sending:** it sends through `smtp:smtp.trailhead.example:587`, logging in as `orders@trailhead.example` with the password `{{mail.password}}`. The sender is `orders@trailhead.example` and the subject is `Order rejected`.\n- **STARTTLS:** `mail.smtp.starttls.enable` and `mail.smtp.starttls.required` are set to `true`. The mail component passes any option starting with `mail.` straight to JavaMail as an extra property. Because `required` is on, the send fails if the server won't upgrade to TLS, instead of sending your login in plain text.\n\n**One risk:** the password is put into the endpoint URI as a property placeholder. If the real password contains `&`, `+` or `%`, those characters could break the URI. In that case, change the value to `\"RAW({{mail.password}})\"`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1278,"cache_read_input_tokens":4587,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1278},"output_tokens":416,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ce32788-7804-4c9e-a1fe-ef71363b3929","uuid":"fedcdeec-c0bb-44ac-bc3f-bd3b945152e4","timestamp":"2026-11-10T16:58:49.582Z","request_id":"req_011CgubvJSxrdkKBdA8EHiQT"}
